cases.answer:
The failure comes from the `SELECT ... INTO` on line 4:

```text
SELECT salary INTO l_salary FROM employees WHERE last_name = p_last_name;
```

`HARNESS_APP.EMPLOYEES.LAST_NAME` is not unique, so `WHERE last_name = p_last_name` can match more than one row. For `SMITH`, it evidently does, and `SELECT ... INTO` raises `ORA-01422: exact fetch returns more than one requested row`.

The safe repair is to make the ambiguity explicit instead of guessing which salary to return. The function below keeps the same interface and raises a named application error when the last name matches multiple employees.

```plsql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION salary_for(p_last_name IN VARCHAR2) RETURN NUMBER IS
  l_salary employees.salary%TYPE;
BEGIN
  SELECT salary INTO l_salary FROM employees WHERE last_name = p_last_name;
  RETURN l_salary;
EXCEPTION
  WHEN TOO_MANY_ROWS THEN
    RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(
      -20001,
      'HARNESS_APP.SALARY_FOR: LAST_NAME is not unique: ' || p_last_name
    );
END salary_for;
```

This proposal has not been compiled. It changes only the exception handling; the original `SELECT ... INTO` and `RETURN` remain as they are.

If the intended business rule is to return one salary per employee, the better design would be to identify the employee by a unique key such as `EMPLOYEE_ID`, but that changes the function’s interface and callers, which are not shown in the context.

